`tempSvg.remove()` on line ~78 is only reached on the happy path. If `configureLabelImages(...)` throws, the temporary SVG element is never removed from `document.body`, leaking a DOM node. Wrap the measurement block in a `try/finally`:
```js
try {
  await configureLabelImages(div.node(), node.label);
  // ... bbox logic ...
} finally {
  tempSvg.remove();
}
```
The same issue exists in `dagre-wrapper/createLabel.js`.

`getConfig()` is called twice within the same synchronous expression to read `.fontSize`. Cache the result in a local variable:
```ts
const cfg = getConfig();
const bodyFontSize = cfg.fontSize ?? window.getComputedStyle(document.body).fontSize;
```
This avoids a redundant config lookup and is consistent with the pattern used elsewhere in the codebase.

The `setTimeout(() => { if (img.complete) setupImage(); })` guard only calls `setupImage` if the image happens to be `complete` when the timer fires. If an already-cached image fires its `load` event synchronously before the event listener is attached, and then `img.complete` is `false` at the time the `setTimeout` fires (edge case in some browsers where `complete` is temporarily reset), the promise would never settle. A more robust pattern is to check `img.complete` *synchronously* right after attaching the listeners, removing the `setTimeout` entirely:
```ts
img.addEventListener('error', setupImage);
img.addEventListener('load', setupImage);
if (img.complete) setupImage();
```
